      "text": "To obtain the rightmost 3 digits of a number $n$ we can compute the number mod 1000.\nTo compute this we can use the modulo exponetiation property which states that $a^{b} \\equiv a^{\\bmod \\phi(n)} \\equiv a^{\\lfloor \\frac{b}{\\phi(n)} \\rfloor \\cdot \\phi(n) + x} \\pmod{n}$  Let $\\phi(n) = n\\left(1- \\frac{1}{p_1^{\\alpha_1}} \\cdots \\frac{1}{p_n^{\\alpha_n}} \\right) $ $(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)$ integers coprime to n and $(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_n)$ positive integers then\n\n$(a^{}_b) \\pmod{ n} = a^{b}_{\\phi(n)}$\n\nTherefore $^51993 = 5^{333}$\n\nWe can then say since the factors in the exponent do not share common factors with the modulo we can mod any clean integer by any modulo with impunity until such point that the power of modulo held is too large which leads to the error(which will never happen in practice) $5^{2000} \\equiv 1 \\ (\\text{mod } 1000)$\n\nAnd so\n\n$(5^{333}) \\pmod{1000} = 5^{1993-333} =5^{1660} \\pmod{1000}$\n\nIf the gcd of 5 and 1000 doesn't divide 1660 then we are done. Because lcm(1660,1000) = 2000.\n\nThe main issues which arise are 5 and 49 are obvious 5 is fairly obvious 45 and 20 might have worked pretty well its just that I keep staring and my mind grinds to a halt. Mean $\\frac{1}{2}(\\text{mod} 20)$ is a fairly obvious congruence to consider. I have never seen $5^{1661}$ be reduced modulo $2000$, so I go ahead and skip it for now.\n\nWe can write $1660 = 20 \\cdot 83$, but 83 is not pretty if we turn to the ring of integers mod $45$ then to reduce, we would have found a power than a multiple of $45$ raised to about $83$ times. There are two generating principles: (1) We can reduce any number in the modulus mod the gcd first and, assuming that gcd divides the exponent, raise any number to any power of the gcd roots. (2) Assuming that the exponents are coprime to all of the prime factors of the modulus, we can compute mod each power $p_i^{\\alpha_i},$ multiplying results by other congruences in each step.\n\nOn this particular problem, we need to find $5^{1993} \\pmod{45}.$  We can reduce the power first, since $1993$ is not divisible by $17$ nor by $4$ at all.  The answer will be an integer power of $5$ more than exactly $83$ times $20$, so we're done.  We already know that $45 = 5 \\cdot 9,$ so we write the problem in the special form $5^{t+83\\cdot 20} \\pmod{45},$ where $t$ is the exponent mod $\\phi(9) = 6.$  [asy]\n\n         import olympiad;\n         import geometry;\n         size(15cm);  draw((303,0)--(270,0)--(270,23)--(290,23)--(303,23)--(303,0)\n          -- cycle,hidden(true));  draw((146,0)--(160,0)--(160,23)--(180,23)--(174,23)\n          --(174,0)--((174,0)+(-18,23))); draw((7,0)--(10,0)--(10,23)--(7,23)--(9,23)\n          --(9,0)(18,23)); pair v = 30.03cm/7cm*(7,0), c = 30.03cm/7cm*(10,0), a\n          = 30.03cm/7cm*(10,23); draw(a--v--c,dotted); draw(c--(c+(-18,23))) ;  draw((293,0)-(19,20)--(279,0)-(14,20)--(51,0)-(-12,20)--(51,23)\n          --(293,23)-(18,20) ) ;  draw((299,0)-(11,23)--(273,0)-(5,23)--(8,0)-(4,23)--(8,23)\n          --(299,23) ) ;  label(\"Split\", (311,0), NW);  label(\"$5^{1993}$\",(303,18),N);  draw(Circle((34,0),0.5)); dot((34,0)); dot((44,0));  label(\"83\",(8.6,5), NE);  label(\"45\",(18.6,5));  label(\"9\",(29,5),SE);  dot(v); dot(c); dot(a); [/asy]  $ 5^{1993} = (5^6)^{332}5^1,$ so the answer is $\\boxed{\\text{Waffle007}}.$  .\n\n\n[Image: https://i.redd.it/85acxq1idla11.png]",
